## Why This Is Asked
This question tests proficiency with partition dynamic programming where subarrays must be non-overlapping and values derive from range statistics. The solution requires careful separation of concerns: precomputing range queries, implementing the DP transition, and reconstructing the solution through pointer tracking.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping contiguous subarrays as partition elements
- Dynamic programming with two-dimensional state tracking
- Range minima preprocessing for O(1) query access
- Solution reconstruction through backpointers
- Complexity optimization through careful algorithm design

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysMin(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;

  // Precompute range minima
  const mins = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let m = Infinity;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      m = Math.min(m, A[j]);
      mins[i][j] = m;
    }
  }
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a less-traveled DP angle: maximizing a sum of subarray-specific statistics (distinct counts) with exact segmentation. Requires careful state design and backtracking for reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix end i and segment count t
- Subarray distinct counts D(p,i-1)
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Time: O(n^2 k), Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDistinctPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // Precompute distinct counts D[l][r]
  const D = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      seen.add(A[r]);
      D[l][r] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const bt = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = D[0][i - 1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 1; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const cand = dp[p][t - 1] + D[p][i - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; bt[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i = n, t = k;
  const segs = [];
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = bt[i][t];
    segs.push([p, i - 1]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  segs.reverse();
  return { best: dp[n][k], segments: segs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) memory?
- How would you handle at most k segments instead of exactly k?
- Can you adapt to streaming data or reconstruct segments efficiently for large n?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests fixed-length path DP and path reconstruction in graphs, a core DP pattern that shows depth beyond standard knapsack.

## Key Concepts
- DP on path length t
- State transition using edges
- Backpointer reconstruction
- Time and space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEdgePath(n, edges, k) {
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) dp[v][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (const e of edges) {
      const {u,v,w} = e;
      const prev = dp[u][t-1];
      if (prev !== NEG) {
        const cand = prev + w;
        if (cand > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = cand;
          par[v][t] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let bestV = 0, best = NEG;
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) if (dp[v][k] > best) { best = dp[v][k]; bestV = v; }
  const path = [];
  let cur = bestV;
  for (let t=k; t>=1; t--) { path.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; }
  path.push(cur);
  path.reverse();
  return {path, weight: best};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this change if edges could have negative weights? How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real DP design with an extra state (discount used) and careful recurrence.

## Key Concepts
- 2D DP with a binary state (discount used or not)
- Transitions combine taking cost with/without discount
- Space optimization using rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostWithDiscount(grid){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  let dp0 = new Array(m).fill(INF);
  let dp1 = new Array(m).fill(INF);
  dp0[0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0] = Math.floor(grid[0][0] / 2);
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
    dp0[j] = grid[0][j] + dp0[j-1];
    dp1[j] = Math.min(grid[0][j] + dp1[j-1], Math.floor(grid[0][j]/2) + dp0[j-1]);
  }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    dp0[0] += grid[i][0];
    dp1[0] = Math.min(grid[i][0] + dp1[0], Math.floor(grid[i][0]/2) + dp0[0-1]);
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const v = grid[i][j];
      dp1[j] = Math.min(v + Math.min(dp1[j], dp1[j-1]), Math.floor(v/2) + Math.min(dp0[j], dp0[j-1]));
      dp0[j] = v + Math.min(dp0[j], dp0[j-1]);
    }
  }
  return dp1[m-1];
}
```

